steps.message:
Do **not** hand-roll a file-system wrapper here — the established **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package already provides `IFileSystem`, covering `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText` and `Directory.Exists`. Reuse it.

### 1. Reference the package and register it

```csharp
// dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 2. Inject IFileSystem and replace the statics

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
    {
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(Path.GetDirectoryName(path)!))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(Path.GetDirectoryName(path)!);
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);
    }

    public string Load(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
}
```

### 3. Test with MockFileSystem

Use `MockFileSystem` from `System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers` — seed it, then assert on what the code read back:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;
using Xunit;

[Fact]
public void Load_ReturnsTheSeededContent()
{
    var fs = new MockFileSystem();
    fs.AddFile("/data/report.txt", new MockFileData("hello"));
    var generator = new ReportGenerator(fs);

    var result = generator.Load("/data/report.txt");

    Assert.Equal("hello", result);
}
```

The test seeds the in-memory file system with known content and asserts the code under test read exactly that back — no touching the real disk. This is why the shared package beats a custom wrapper: the mock is maintained for you.
